1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
From the unthinkable...

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Our recorders survive in the worst of crashes.

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:09,000
...to the unimaginable...

4
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:13,000
There is nothing that this cannot protect you from.

5
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:17,000
They're meant to survive, even if we don't.

6
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:23,000
They could be here for centuries, even though we use them every day,

7
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:26,000
and outlast us long into the afterlife.

8
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:29,000
Metal caskets will last years into the future.

9
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:34,000
From eternity to liberty, some things just have to endure.

10
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Stick around.

11
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:40,000
It's built to last on Modern Marvels.

12
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Whether it's structures expected to endure through the ages,

13
00:00:56,001 --> 00:00:59,340
or devices we count on when we need them most,

14
00:00:59,440 --> 00:01:03,600
few things on Earth are truly built to last.

15
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:07,140
But perhaps the very symbol of long-lasting innovation

16
00:01:07,240 --> 00:01:10,040
is found inside one of man's greatest inventions.

17
00:01:17,660 --> 00:01:19,760
Whether you're a fan of the friendly skies

18
00:01:19,860 --> 00:01:23,500
or afraid to fly, most of us try not to dwell

19
00:01:23,501 --> 00:01:26,500
try not to dwell on the idea of a plane crashing.

20
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:33,500
But for the experts at L3 Communications,

21
00:01:33,501 --> 00:01:35,500
it's a threat they think about every day.

22
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:40,500
At their headquarters in Sarasota, Florida,

23
00:01:40,501 --> 00:01:43,500
the mission is to make sure at least part of the plane,

24
00:01:43,501 --> 00:01:47,500
the black box, will survive a potentially catastrophic event.

25
00:01:47,501 --> 00:01:50,500
The crash survival tests are meant to simulate

26
00:01:50,501 --> 00:01:52,500
an actual crash event of an airplane.

27
00:01:53,500 --> 00:02:00,500
L3 engineers manufacture and test these icons of survival.

28
00:02:00,501 --> 00:02:06,500
Large passenger planes contain not one, but two of the widely recognized devices.

29
00:02:06,501 --> 00:02:10,500
In commercial air carriers you'll find both a cockpit voice recorder, like this shorter box,

30
00:02:10,501 --> 00:02:14,500
and a flight data recorder, like this longer box.

31
00:02:14,501 --> 00:02:19,500
The first thing about black boxes that's clear is they're not black at all.

32
00:02:19,501 --> 00:02:22,800
The recorder is bright orange per the specifications,

33
00:02:23,120 --> 00:02:27,120
and it's done that way so that the recorder can be easily found in the wreckage.

34
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:31,660
It's believed by some that the misnomer might refer to the charring

35
00:02:31,661 --> 00:02:37,020
that occurs in post-accident fires or the color of early recorders.

36
00:02:44,020 --> 00:02:48,000
Just as aircraft are pushed to the limit to test their crash survivability,

37
00:02:48,001 --> 00:02:51,000
So are their black boxes.

38
00:02:51,001 --> 00:02:55,000
In today's test, experts are making sure a cockpit voice

39
00:02:55,001 --> 00:03:00,000
recorder can take a beating in the lab before it ever boards a flight.

40
00:03:00,001 --> 00:03:05,000
The static crush takes the unit and places it in this machine

41
00:03:05,001 --> 00:03:13,000
and applies 5,000-pound crushing force to simulate crush of large

42
00:03:13,001 --> 00:03:17,000
components of an airplane that may fall off during an accident and

43
00:03:17,001 --> 00:03:19,960
and land on the recorder.

44
00:03:19,961 --> 00:03:23,500
All large commercial aircraft and some smaller planes

45
00:03:23,501 --> 00:03:26,720
must be equipped with black boxes on board.

46
00:03:26,721 --> 00:03:28,940
When disaster strikes, the devices

47
00:03:28,941 --> 00:03:33,720
are expected to survive with critical data intact.

48
00:03:33,721 --> 00:03:36,280
The flight data recorder tracks key parameters

49
00:03:36,281 --> 00:03:38,480
from different parts of the plane,

50
00:03:38,481 --> 00:03:43,820
like airspeed, wing flap position, vertical acceleration,

51
00:03:43,821 --> 00:03:46,860
and engine speed.

52
00:03:46,861 --> 00:03:49,180
The audio is cockpit audio.

53
00:03:51,520 --> 00:03:55,120
The cockpit voice recorder captures crew conversations

54
00:03:55,121 --> 00:03:57,800
and any unusual noises in flight,

55
00:03:58,320 --> 00:04:00,660
thanks to microphones positioned around the cockpit.

56
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:05,160
The cockpit voice recorder itself

57
00:04:05,161 --> 00:04:08,180
is installed alongside the flight data recorder

58
00:04:08,181 --> 00:04:10,940
in what's considered the toughest part of the plane.

59
00:04:11,760 --> 00:04:15,120
The tail has usually spared a lot of the trauma

60
00:04:15,121 --> 00:04:16,680
of the rest of the accident.

61
00:04:16,860 --> 00:04:20,460
That is the portion that historically has survived the crash

62
00:04:20,461 --> 00:04:22,460
better than any other section of the airplane.

63
00:04:25,740 --> 00:04:28,320
To make sure these devices are battle-tested,

64
00:04:28,900 --> 00:04:32,840
the next stop on the torture tour is the pin penetration test.

65
00:04:34,180 --> 00:04:36,600
We'll take this 500-pound weight,

66
00:04:37,260 --> 00:04:39,840
insert this quarter-inch hardened steel pin in the bottom,

67
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:42,340
hoist it up 10 feet,

68
00:04:42,660 --> 00:04:45,860
and then drop it directly on the bullseye here,

69
00:04:45,861 --> 00:04:48,720
in an attempt to penetrate the crash housing.

70
00:04:50,420 --> 00:04:52,700
The test is meant to simulate the debris

71
00:04:52,701 --> 00:04:54,820
that could fly at the device during a crash.

72
00:04:56,360 --> 00:04:59,160
A special electrically charged rope is used

73
00:04:59,161 --> 00:05:02,980
so that when it severs, it delivers a perfectly centered hit.

74
00:05:03,500 --> 00:05:05,840
We take a nichrome wire and weave it through it,

75
00:05:06,140 --> 00:05:09,640
and we use a DC current to heat it up

76
00:05:09,641 --> 00:05:11,300
and melt it to a breaking point

77
00:05:11,301 --> 00:05:14,940
so that the weight drops center

78
00:05:14,941 --> 00:05:16,200
over where we had it set at.

79
00:05:17,740 --> 00:05:22,580
The ultimate question is whether the black box's tiny but powerful memory module,

80
00:05:23,220 --> 00:05:26,000
positioned right in the bullseye, can survive.

81
00:05:26,660 --> 00:05:29,100
It's where all the critical information is stored.

82
00:05:29,840 --> 00:05:32,680
But how to capture that data has evolved over the years.

83
00:05:33,760 --> 00:05:38,120
Early black boxes recorded data onto foil and later magnetic tape.

84
00:05:38,500 --> 00:05:42,280
But moving parts raised the danger for a malfunction in a crash.

85
00:05:44,940 --> 00:05:52,120
Today, solid state recorders developed by L3 that rely on memory boards instead help

86
00:05:52,121 --> 00:05:55,660
increase their chance of survival.

87
00:05:55,661 --> 00:06:01,000
Black boxes, whether the flight data recorder or the shorter cockpit voice recorder, have

88
00:06:01,001 --> 00:06:03,380
tough aluminum shells.

89
00:06:03,381 --> 00:06:08,460
But even stronger stainless steel or titanium protect the all-important memory module.

90
00:06:08,461 --> 00:06:12,960
The primary goal of the recorder is to protect that data.

91
00:06:18,360 --> 00:06:23,000
Back at the test lab, the recorder awaits its fate in a sandbox below.

92
00:06:24,320 --> 00:06:25,420
And in an instant.

93
00:06:31,180 --> 00:06:33,920
500 pounds were no match for the box.

94
00:06:34,440 --> 00:06:37,480
We hit our mark right in the center of the crash housing

95
00:06:37,481 --> 00:06:42,780
with the quarter-inch steel pin, and we don't have any penetration.

96
00:06:43,660 --> 00:06:48,380
At L3, the tougher the testing, the more reliable the crash data will be.

97
00:06:51,420 --> 00:06:56,120
In high-profile plane crashes, it's often the recovery of the black boxes

98
00:06:56,121 --> 00:06:58,200
that propels the investigation.

99
00:06:59,440 --> 00:07:01,620
Plane crash rates remain remarkably low.

100
00:07:02,420 --> 00:07:06,920
Only two-and-a-half accidents occur for every one million commercial flights worldwide.

101
00:07:07,480 --> 00:07:16,220
But in those that are catastrophic, the black boxes must often survive one of their most destructive foes, fire.

102
00:07:19,220 --> 00:07:24,560
This test is to simulate an airplane crash where high-temperature fire resulted.

103
00:07:25,340 --> 00:07:29,700
The test consists of three burners that you see around the recorder.

104
00:07:30,480 --> 00:07:36,020
Those burners are set to produce a flame temperature of 1,100 degrees C.

105
00:07:37,480 --> 00:07:45,480
The black box must withstand the heat for the one-hour test.

106
00:07:49,200 --> 00:07:54,700
As the aluminum housing ultimately melts away, the stainless steel case endures.

107
00:07:54,701 --> 00:08:08,701
That data is used post-accident to investigate the cause

108
00:08:09,880 --> 00:08:13,500
and to determine how that accident may have been prevented in the future.

109
00:08:14,200 --> 00:08:18,780
With the fire test complete, the cockpit voice recorder may be charred,

110
00:08:19,140 --> 00:08:20,720
but the data is safe.

111
00:08:20,721 --> 00:08:25,520
You can see that it's still in good shape.

112
00:08:25,620 --> 00:08:27,180
All the paint has been burned off of it.

113
00:08:27,220 --> 00:08:28,420
The labels have been burned off.

114
00:08:28,680 --> 00:08:31,300
But it looks fairly intact.

115
00:08:33,400 --> 00:08:38,880
Beyond fire, black boxes must also endure one of the toughest environments for any crash,

116
00:08:39,560 --> 00:08:40,640
a loss at sea.

117
00:08:41,700 --> 00:08:45,180
One of the test sequences is the deep sea pressure test.

118
00:08:45,320 --> 00:08:48,580
We pressurize it to an equivalent depth of 20,000 feet.

119
00:08:48,581 --> 00:08:55,580
In at-sea crashes, the device's underwater locator beacon emits an ultrasonic pinging

120
00:08:55,581 --> 00:09:00,440
tone for 30 days after a crash.

121
00:09:00,441 --> 00:09:02,440
But there are limitations.

122
00:09:02,441 --> 00:09:08,420
Debris and even water salinity levels can obscure the beacon's tone.

123
00:09:08,421 --> 00:09:15,220
And in the infamous 1996 crash of TWA Flight 800 off Long Island, New York, the cockpit

124
00:09:15,221 --> 00:09:20,960
voice recorder was recovered, but the locator beacon was missing entirely, torn off in the

125
00:09:20,961 --> 00:09:21,661
crash.

126
00:09:21,662 --> 00:09:27,920
But the recorders themselves are consistently performing in the toughest of environments.

127
00:09:27,921 --> 00:09:34,940
Our current recorder so far has an unblemished track record in data recovery and audio recovery.

128
00:09:35,580 --> 00:09:39,340
Our recorders are built to survive even the worst of crashes.

129
00:09:39,341 --> 00:09:46,940
Next generation ideas include a cockpit video recorder to capture critical pre-crash moments.

130
00:09:49,140 --> 00:09:55,300
Like planes, many new American cars have their own version of black box technology,

131
00:09:55,680 --> 00:09:58,480
designed to withstand the cruelest disasters.

132
00:10:04,180 --> 00:10:07,500
At General Motors testing ground in Milford, Michigan,

133
00:10:07,501 --> 00:10:11,060
cars must prove they can survive the toughest safety tests.

134
00:10:12,100 --> 00:10:16,600
And one small device inside is built to stand up to the worst of crashes.

135
00:10:17,860 --> 00:10:20,260
This is an example of the SDM.

136
00:10:20,740 --> 00:10:22,280
This is the brains of the system.

137
00:10:23,360 --> 00:10:28,720
The SDM, or Sensing and Diagnostic Module, is the car's version of the black box.

138
00:10:29,280 --> 00:10:34,000
It monitors the car's performance data and controls airbag deployment in a crash.

139
00:10:34,001 --> 00:10:39,314
Though not required to by law, GM and several other major U.S.

140
00:10:39,315 --> 00:10:41,800
carmakers use the crash-ready

141
00:10:41,801 --> 00:10:42,532
technology.

142
00:10:42,533 --> 00:10:48,120
From its floor-mounted position in the front part of a vehicle, the SDM communicates

143
00:10:48,121 --> 00:10:51,184
with sensors on the front and sides of a car.

144
00:10:51,185 --> 00:10:54,520
It receives the signals from all of these sensors

145
00:10:54,521 --> 00:10:59,060
and makes decisions on which airbags to deploy and when.

146
00:10:59,061 --> 00:11:02,060
To ensure its survival, the device is

147
00:11:02,061 --> 00:11:05,680
made from tough nylon plastic and is placed near the passenger

148
00:11:05,681 --> 00:11:09,460
area, the most protected part of the car.

149
00:11:09,461 --> 00:11:13,720
Acceleration forces that are generated in severe crashes

150
00:11:13,721 --> 00:11:15,920
can be over 100 Gs.

151
00:11:15,921 --> 00:11:19,620
That's 100 times the force of gravity in this area.

152
00:11:19,621 --> 00:11:22,100
Certainly, it's an area that we don't want to have damage

153
00:11:22,101 --> 00:11:24,600
because this is where our customers are sitting.

154
00:11:24,601 --> 00:11:27,540
And when a crash occurs, the SDM functions

155
00:11:27,541 --> 00:11:31,760
similarly to a black box as it captures performance data.

156
00:11:31,761 --> 00:11:33,920
but with some key differences.

157
00:11:33,921 --> 00:11:36,960
Whereas the flight data recorder is always recording data,

158
00:11:36,961 --> 00:11:39,020
our recorder is only recording in the event

159
00:11:39,021 --> 00:11:40,880
of a certain crash, for instance, something

160
00:11:40,881 --> 00:11:42,800
above five miles per hour.

161
00:11:42,801 --> 00:11:45,320
And it will record data for roughly five seconds

162
00:11:45,321 --> 00:11:46,220
prior to the impact.

163
00:11:51,420 --> 00:11:55,600
Plane crashes, thankfully, are incredibly rare events.

164
00:11:55,601 --> 00:11:59,060
But car accidents claim the lives of some 40,000

165
00:11:59,061 --> 00:12:01,340
Americans each year.

166
00:12:01,341 --> 00:12:04,100
Testing vehicles to outlast the devastation

167
00:12:04,101 --> 00:12:06,780
is a constant challenge.

168
00:12:06,781 --> 00:12:09,840
This car wears the scars from a front barrier test

169
00:12:09,841 --> 00:12:12,580
at 25 miles per hour.

170
00:12:12,581 --> 00:12:15,140
As you can see, the front airbags deployed.

171
00:12:15,141 --> 00:12:17,580
Sensing diagnostic module is in an area that

172
00:12:17,581 --> 00:12:22,920
received minimal damage and certainly looks well protected.

173
00:12:22,921 --> 00:12:26,780
Post-crash, the SDM is connected to a computer.

174
00:12:26,781 --> 00:12:29,860
The surviving data is analyzed for clues,

175
00:12:29,861 --> 00:12:32,900
such as speed or brake application before impact.

176
00:12:33,420 --> 00:12:35,440
What we have is a record of the crash,

177
00:12:35,620 --> 00:12:37,580
indicating how the airbag system functioned,

178
00:12:37,581 --> 00:12:39,460
as well as some of the pre-crash data

179
00:12:39,461 --> 00:12:42,420
that the vehicle experienced prior to the impact.

180
00:12:45,820 --> 00:12:47,880
A safe car ride is important,

181
00:12:48,200 --> 00:12:50,000
as long as what's beneath your wheels

182
00:12:50,001 --> 00:12:51,860
has also been built to last.

183
00:12:52,280 --> 00:12:53,620
But peel back the asphalt,

184
00:12:54,140 --> 00:12:57,060
and you'll see how something that could last more than 500 years

185
00:12:57,061 --> 00:12:59,620
is carving out a real future on our roads.

186
00:12:59,860 --> 00:13:02,800
Built to Last will return on Modern Marvels.

187
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:07,660
We now return to Built to Last on Modern Marvels.

188
00:13:10,700 --> 00:13:13,020
When it comes to having a long lifespan,

189
00:13:13,560 --> 00:13:17,000
few things can compare to what most of us know as Styrofoam.

190
00:13:17,340 --> 00:13:21,600
But this familiar foam product actually has many different names and uses.

191
00:13:22,920 --> 00:13:26,100
And whether you're getting takeout or your morning coffee,

192
00:13:26,101 --> 00:13:29,740
the squeaky white containers are universally known for their durability.

193
00:13:29,860 --> 00:13:36,860
Foam packaging, derived from petroleum-based plastic, does not biodegrade or disintegrate

194
00:13:36,861 --> 00:13:41,300
and can remain in landfills for centuries.

195
00:13:41,301 --> 00:13:46,300
That longevity has raised some environmental concerns, but it's exactly why foam is being

196
00:13:46,301 --> 00:13:51,380
used in surprising ways all around us.

197
00:13:51,381 --> 00:13:59,040
Foam has spread beneath our feet, to roads, bridges, even airport runways, and it's built

198
00:13:59,041 --> 00:14:02,529
to last. EPS foam is incredibly strong.

199
00:14:02,530 --> 00:14:07,360
It has a PSI, pounds per square inch, of 25 pounds for

200
00:14:07,361 --> 00:14:12,760
a compressive resistance, which means that a square foot of foam is 144 square inches.

201
00:14:12,761 --> 00:14:13,461
It will

202
00:14:13,462 --> 00:14:15,386
hold approximately 3,600 pounds.

203
00:14:15,387 --> 00:14:19,280
It can hold tremendous amounts of weight before it fails.

204
00:14:21,920 --> 00:14:28,240
At Harbor Foam in Granville, Michigan, you can almost imagine a world entirely made of foam.

205
00:14:29,040 --> 00:14:38,040
EPS foam, or expanded polystyrene by its more formal name, is a moldable plastic when heated.

206
00:14:38,041 --> 00:14:45,040
It's most recognizable in packaging for food or fragile purchases, like electronics.

207
00:14:45,041 --> 00:14:53,040
But here, foam is really coming to life to help support, literally, the nation's roads.

208
00:14:53,041 --> 00:15:00,880
The 1956 Federal Aid Highway Act, creating the nation's highway system, called it essential to the national interest.

209
00:15:01,380 --> 00:15:03,660
And it's expected to be there when we need it.

210
00:15:05,440 --> 00:15:15,140
Today, the nation's highways and roads are used by hundreds of millions of vehicles a year, resulting in annual maintenance costs of over $40 trillion.

211
00:15:15,141 --> 00:15:20,140
Block by block, in projects across 40 states,

212
00:15:20,240 --> 00:15:22,740
foam is paving the way to toughen those roads

213
00:15:22,840 --> 00:15:24,140
for the long haul.

214
00:15:24,240 --> 00:15:29,180
EPS foam has replaced soil where they have unsuitable soil.

215
00:15:29,280 --> 00:15:31,180
If it's muck or if it's swampy land,

216
00:15:31,280 --> 00:15:35,100
the road's always kind of giving away after four or five years.

217
00:15:35,200 --> 00:15:37,500
They just decided to pull all the old soil out,

218
00:15:37,600 --> 00:15:40,240
and they'll put EPS foam down there.

219
00:15:40,340 --> 00:15:43,740
In road projects, on top of a gravel base,

220
00:15:43,741 --> 00:15:48,740
At the gravel base, layers of the special geofoam are stacked,

221
00:15:48,741 --> 00:15:51,740
anywhere from two to 20 layers tall.

222
00:15:51,741 --> 00:15:57,740
A rubber membrane is laid over the geofoam to protect it from any road liquid or fuel spills.

223
00:15:57,741 --> 00:16:03,740
A final layer of soil is placed on top before the asphalt makes the road complete.

224
00:16:03,741 --> 00:16:08,740
Dirt fill is about 100 to 120 pounds per cubic foot,

225
00:16:08,741 --> 00:16:11,740
compared to foam, which is 1 to 2 pounds per cubic foot.

226
00:16:13,740 --> 00:16:20,800
A simple demonstration outside Harbor Foam's plant shows just how durable this seemingly

227
00:16:20,801 --> 00:16:24,420
lightweight material really is.

228
00:16:24,421 --> 00:16:29,580
These small foam ramps will prove they're all muscle.

229
00:16:29,581 --> 00:16:35,800
So this foam here is 96% air.

230
00:16:35,801 --> 00:16:40,920
It weighs less than 10 pounds and it's capable of holding a truck that is in excess of 7,200

231
00:16:40,921 --> 00:16:42,920
and is very, very strong, as you can see.

232
00:16:48,920 --> 00:16:51,560
The foam has the same properties as the packing peanuts

233
00:16:51,660 --> 00:16:53,960
we're used to seeing all over our floors,

234
00:16:54,060 --> 00:16:57,160
just a more compacted form with less static

235
00:16:57,260 --> 00:16:58,880
and a different name.

236
00:16:58,980 --> 00:17:01,080
Most people refer to this as Styrofoam.

237
00:17:01,180 --> 00:17:03,280
Styrofoam is a brand name by Dow.

238
00:17:03,380 --> 00:17:05,880
This is actually expanded polystyrene,

239
00:17:05,980 --> 00:17:08,220
but just like everything else, like Kleenex,

240
00:17:08,320 --> 00:17:10,920
it's a brand name, and it just sticks with it.

241
00:17:10,921 --> 00:17:15,920
So how does this chameleon-like wonder come to life?

242
00:17:15,921 --> 00:17:18,920
It starts small.

243
00:17:18,921 --> 00:17:20,920
These bees, before they come to us,

244
00:17:20,921 --> 00:17:22,920
they're processed in reactors,

245
00:17:22,921 --> 00:17:25,920
and they're 15,000-gallon reactors,

246
00:17:25,921 --> 00:17:27,920
and styrene is a liquid.

247
00:17:27,921 --> 00:17:30,920
They dump approximately 6,000 pounds of styrene in here,

248
00:17:30,921 --> 00:17:33,920
and they dump about 3,000 pounds of water,

249
00:17:33,921 --> 00:17:35,920
and it's just like oil and water.

250
00:17:35,921 --> 00:17:36,920
They don't mix.

251
00:17:36,921 --> 00:17:39,920
The faster you agitate it, the smaller the balls you get,

252
00:17:39,921 --> 00:17:41,920
And that's where we get these small beads.

253
00:17:43,920 --> 00:17:46,920
The beads then begin a multi-step process,

254
00:17:46,921 --> 00:17:48,920
which starts at the pre-expander.

255
00:17:49,920 --> 00:17:53,920
It's here that the beads begin their amazing physical transformation,

256
00:17:53,921 --> 00:17:58,920
when the pentane inside them changes from a liquid to a gas.

257
00:17:58,921 --> 00:18:00,920
The polystyrene sits on the floor,

258
00:18:00,921 --> 00:18:02,920
and the steam comes up from the bottom of the floor,

259
00:18:02,921 --> 00:18:05,920
and that's what reacts with that pentane.

260
00:18:05,921 --> 00:18:07,920
The beads get conditioned, they get worked,

261
00:18:07,921 --> 00:18:13,060
they get work, that bead gets soft and it starts to expand with that pentane gas releasing

262
00:18:13,061 --> 00:18:19,717
the beads expand. The beads ultimately grow to 40 times their original size.

263
00:18:19,718 --> 00:18:20,680
The machine

264
00:18:20,681 --> 00:18:26,311
can be set for different recipes based on the amount of beads used.

265
00:18:26,312 --> 00:18:27,740
A lighter mixture

266
00:18:27,741 --> 00:18:32,772
would be made for packing materials, a denser, tougher one for road use.

267
00:18:32,773 --> 00:18:34,380
The higher the density,

268
00:18:34,381 --> 00:18:36,340
the stronger it is, the more weight there is,

269
00:18:36,520 --> 00:18:37,960
the more wear and tear it can have.

270
00:18:40,440 --> 00:18:43,220
The beads then take a break in storage silos

271
00:18:43,221 --> 00:18:45,920
where they lose some of their pentane gas,

272
00:18:46,400 --> 00:18:48,860
making them easier to mold into solid forms.

273
00:18:52,820 --> 00:18:55,180
The block mold with its internal vacuum

274
00:18:55,181 --> 00:18:57,040
is where all the action happens

275
00:18:57,041 --> 00:18:58,880
for the foam's final metamorphosis.

276
00:19:00,420 --> 00:19:02,160
It's where the hyperactive beads

277
00:19:02,161 --> 00:19:03,840
start to take on their new role.

278
00:19:04,380 --> 00:19:06,380
as usable, durable foam.

279
00:19:07,720 --> 00:19:09,460
We're going to stick it in the block mold,

280
00:19:09,500 --> 00:19:13,920
and we're going to make a 2 foot by 4 foot by 16 foot long block.

281
00:19:15,840 --> 00:19:17,140
The beads are going to get together,

282
00:19:17,280 --> 00:19:19,840
and when you hit it with heat, steam, it's going to want to expand.

283
00:19:20,160 --> 00:19:21,400
But there's nowhere for it to go.

284
00:19:21,720 --> 00:19:24,040
So what it's going to do is it's going to mold itself together.

285
00:19:24,260 --> 00:19:25,520
It's going to fuse itself together,

286
00:19:25,521 --> 00:19:28,200
and you're going to have a finished block when you're done.

287
00:19:29,960 --> 00:19:34,240
The final blocks often tower over the workers who move them.

288
00:19:34,380 --> 00:19:41,260
They are now tough enough to support major highway projects.

289
00:19:42,840 --> 00:19:47,180
Or they can be sliced down thin enough for delicate packing material.

290
00:19:50,180 --> 00:19:55,100
Another reason the finished product is built to last is its recyclable properties.

291
00:19:56,300 --> 00:20:01,880
We recycle probably over a half million pounds of EPS a year in our facility.

292
00:20:01,881 --> 00:20:03,880
We take it back from consumers.

293
00:20:03,881 --> 00:20:05,880
We got a lot of post-consumer product here.

294
00:20:05,881 --> 00:20:07,880
Take it back from appliance stores.

295
00:20:07,881 --> 00:20:10,880
We recycle 100% of our stuff.

296
00:20:10,881 --> 00:20:17,880
Over 69 million pounds of EPS foam was recycled nationwide in 2008.

297
00:20:19,880 --> 00:20:23,880
And it can be reused in some rather unexpected ways.

298
00:20:23,881 --> 00:20:28,880
Recycled foam is found in CD cases and even plastic hangers.

299
00:20:28,881 --> 00:20:32,220
It's melted down and it's got a mold and they inject the

300
00:20:32,221 --> 00:20:36,880
melted foam right in there and they cool it down and out pops a hanger.

301
00:20:36,881 --> 00:20:43,880
But foam's long lasting use in roads and bridges could be its most valuable legacy.

302
00:20:43,881 --> 00:20:48,029
It doesn't disintegrate, it does not break down. It's just like it was.

303
00:20:48,030 --> 00:20:52,880
If you dig it up 20 years from now, it's the same as when you put it in the ground.

304
00:20:52,881 --> 00:20:55,880
Foam is here to stay. It's built to last.

305
00:20:56,800 --> 00:20:58,720
Foam may be built for survival,

306
00:20:59,080 --> 00:21:01,780
but it's not the only thing made to go underground

307
00:21:01,781 --> 00:21:03,180
that's expected to last.

308
00:21:04,560 --> 00:21:06,300
Perhaps for eternity.

309
00:21:09,340 --> 00:21:11,960
The Eiffel Tower, built as an exhibit

310
00:21:11,961 --> 00:21:14,320
for the 1889 World's Fair in Paris,

311
00:21:14,700 --> 00:21:16,860
was expected to last for just 20 years.

312
00:21:17,580 --> 00:21:19,500
The Iron Marvel has outlived predictions

313
00:21:19,501 --> 00:21:21,100
by more than a century.

314
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,420
We now return to Built to Last

315
00:21:24,421 --> 00:21:25,520
on Modern Marvel.

316
00:21:28,120 --> 00:21:30,220
Building things that are meant to last

317
00:21:30,221 --> 00:21:32,360
has been one of man's eternal quests.

318
00:21:33,920 --> 00:21:37,700
The pyramids, at nearly 5,000 years old,

319
00:21:37,960 --> 00:21:40,100
are perhaps the world's most famous example.

320
00:21:41,800 --> 00:21:43,940
Ancient Egyptians hoped the colossal structures

321
00:21:43,941 --> 00:21:46,200
would protect the mummified remains inside,

322
00:21:46,860 --> 00:21:47,680
into the afterlife.

323
00:21:49,900 --> 00:21:53,320
Still today, if anything is expected to last forever,

324
00:21:53,321 --> 00:21:55,660
it's your final resting place.

325
00:21:57,220 --> 00:21:59,200
Just ask Thacker Caskets,

326
00:21:59,500 --> 00:22:02,000
which turns out 50,000 units a year.

327
00:22:03,180 --> 00:22:04,860
It's an age-old product,

328
00:22:05,100 --> 00:22:07,500
but modern technology is also critical

329
00:22:07,501 --> 00:22:09,320
to this undertaking.

330
00:22:09,760 --> 00:22:11,600
The days of the old wooden pine box

331
00:22:11,601 --> 00:22:12,580
are basically gone.

332
00:22:12,900 --> 00:22:14,940
Most families today are looking for something

333
00:22:14,941 --> 00:22:17,520
with durability, and the metal caskets provide that.

334
00:22:18,840 --> 00:22:20,940
Just as longer-lasting metal

335
00:22:20,941 --> 00:22:22,040
has taken over wood,

336
00:22:22,740 --> 00:22:25,300
Caskets have long replaced coffins in the U.S.

337
00:22:26,100 --> 00:22:30,540
The six-sided coffin style became nearly extinct in the 1930s

338
00:22:30,541 --> 00:22:34,460
when the rectangular, more decorative casket grew in popularity

339
00:22:34,461 --> 00:22:36,940
and became what we recognize today.

340
00:22:37,940 --> 00:22:41,520
More than 60% of caskets sold in the U.S. today

341
00:22:41,521 --> 00:22:43,620
are constructed from sheet steel.

342
00:22:44,240 --> 00:22:46,480
Even without an inhabitant inside,

343
00:22:46,960 --> 00:22:49,940
the metal carries an average weight of 150 pounds,

344
00:22:50,500 --> 00:22:51,660
providing added heft.

345
00:22:52,040 --> 00:22:56,300
We use 20-gauge, 18-gauge, and 16-gauge cold-rolled steel.

346
00:22:56,920 --> 00:23:00,820
The 20-gauge caskets are the lighter-gauge material that we make,

347
00:23:00,920 --> 00:23:01,980
but they're the most popular.

348
00:23:02,640 --> 00:23:08,000
They generally give good durability and protection for very good value.

349
00:23:09,300 --> 00:23:14,360
Today's process for burying the dead is as automated as any American factory.

350
00:23:14,880 --> 00:23:20,040
We bring in metal components, tops, sides, ends, bottoms.

351
00:23:20,041 --> 00:23:22,580
We assemble these components, we weld them,

352
00:23:22,680 --> 00:23:24,780
and place them into production.

353
00:23:24,880 --> 00:23:28,280
We make approximately 270 caskets a day that are finished

354
00:23:28,380 --> 00:23:29,980
and ready to be shipped out of our plants

355
00:23:30,080 --> 00:23:33,980
into distributors and or funeral homes.

356
00:23:34,080 --> 00:23:37,400
The simple metal boxes soon take on their iconic shape

357
00:23:37,500 --> 00:23:40,000
once their lids are attached.

358
00:23:40,100 --> 00:23:42,100
After the casket shell is assembled,

359
00:23:42,200 --> 00:23:44,700
the metal surface is cleaned and treated

360
00:23:44,800 --> 00:23:47,140
to protect from future degradation.

361
00:23:47,240 --> 00:23:49,240
It washes all of the oils off.

362
00:23:49,241 --> 00:23:51,240
It washes all of the dirt and debris off.

363
00:23:51,241 --> 00:23:54,240
Then it applies a phosphate coating,

364
00:23:54,241 --> 00:23:57,240
which keeps the casket from corroding or rusting.

365
00:23:57,241 --> 00:24:01,240
The caskets are carefully hand-inspected for any defects.

366
00:24:01,241 --> 00:24:05,240
I'm one of the many quality control persons that's here.

367
00:24:05,241 --> 00:24:10,240
What I do, I check for dents, stretches, bad whale, good whale.

368
00:24:10,241 --> 00:24:14,240
Anything wrong with the casket at all, I check all of it.

369
00:24:14,241 --> 00:24:18,240
Any flaws are addressed before a casket continues its life

370
00:24:18,241 --> 00:24:22,580
life on the assembly line.

371
00:24:22,581 --> 00:24:25,580
Customer preference is key in the funeral business,

372
00:24:25,581 --> 00:24:28,380
so a variety of colors are offered.

373
00:24:28,381 --> 00:24:33,020
It's the same paint used on cars to ensure a durable finish.

374
00:24:33,021 --> 00:24:34,680
This is our paint booth.

375
00:24:34,681 --> 00:24:39,800
We spray a automotive grade paint on all of our caskets.

376
00:24:39,801 --> 00:24:42,000
We use a 13 different color palette

377
00:24:42,001 --> 00:24:44,400
that we paint our caskets with.

378
00:24:44,401 --> 00:24:47,800
It's very durable, very high end paint,

379
00:24:47,801 --> 00:24:48,840
very high gloss.

380
00:24:49,380 --> 00:24:52,000
It gives us a real long-lasting finish.

381
00:24:55,320 --> 00:24:59,060
Next, the caskets travel through an infrared 300-degree

382
00:24:59,061 --> 00:25:01,560
Fahrenheit oven where the paint is cured,

383
00:25:02,480 --> 00:25:05,860
bringing them one step closer to being six feet under.

384
00:25:08,200 --> 00:25:10,740
Thacker provides their caskets to some of the more than

385
00:25:10,741 --> 00:25:13,340
22,000 funeral homes in the U.S.

386
00:25:14,160 --> 00:25:17,000
But today's funeral industry is rooted partly

387
00:25:17,001 --> 00:25:19,820
in one of America's most violent periods.

388
00:25:20,640 --> 00:25:24,540
It was the Civil War, with its 600,000 killed,

389
00:25:24,920 --> 00:25:27,480
that changed the way Americans treated their dead.

390
00:25:28,500 --> 00:25:31,580
With the deceased being returned to families far from the battlefield,

391
00:25:32,380 --> 00:25:34,560
the need arose to preserve the body longer.

392
00:25:35,880 --> 00:25:39,300
The front lines were soon peppered with experts in embalming,

393
00:25:39,840 --> 00:25:43,060
the process of replacing the body's blood with chemicals

394
00:25:43,061 --> 00:25:45,380
that helped preserve the skin and organs.

395
00:25:45,381 --> 00:25:49,380
Embalming was a very underused science

396
00:25:49,381 --> 00:25:52,380
until the time of the Civil War.

397
00:25:52,381 --> 00:25:54,380
It was actually fairly experimental.

398
00:25:54,381 --> 00:25:56,380
Once the Civil War came about

399
00:25:56,381 --> 00:25:59,380
and there was a desire for families to get the bodies

400
00:25:59,381 --> 00:26:01,380
of their young soldiers back home,

401
00:26:01,381 --> 00:26:05,380
that's what really spurred embalming in the United States.

402
00:26:05,381 --> 00:26:09,380
President Abraham Lincoln was a key proponent

403
00:26:09,381 --> 00:26:12,380
in battlefield embalming and underwent the procedure

404
00:26:12,381 --> 00:26:15,980
the procedure after his own death in 1865.

405
00:26:15,981 --> 00:26:18,980
His funeral caravan allowed seven million Americans

406
00:26:18,981 --> 00:26:22,940
to see his embalmed body and helped spread the practice

407
00:26:22,941 --> 00:26:25,220
to civilian burials.

408
00:26:25,221 --> 00:26:29,440
People were in awe, and they marveled at the fact

409
00:26:29,441 --> 00:26:33,500
that this body could be preserved this long.

410
00:26:33,501 --> 00:26:36,060
That spread the idea of embalming.

411
00:26:36,061 --> 00:26:39,340
I want that for my family.

412
00:26:39,341 --> 00:26:42,520
Embalming meant families could even delay burials

413
00:26:42,521 --> 00:26:43,840
for post-mortem portraits.

414
00:26:46,520 --> 00:26:50,420
For the modern burial, the 1.6 million caskets produced

415
00:26:50,421 --> 00:26:54,280
annually in the US have durability in common.

416
00:26:54,281 --> 00:26:58,940
Many models feature a special seal for extra peace of mind.

417
00:26:58,941 --> 00:27:01,860
The number of our caskets that we make are gasketed caskets.

418
00:27:01,861 --> 00:27:04,860
These caskets have a gasket that

419
00:27:04,861 --> 00:27:06,380
goes between the lid of the casket

420
00:27:06,381 --> 00:27:11,620
the body of the casket, giving it a good, tight fit.

421
00:27:11,621 --> 00:27:17,320
Final touches are added to give the cold metal some personality.

422
00:27:17,321 --> 00:27:22,820
For those seeking some added style and longevity, copper and bronze models feature a higher price

423
00:27:22,821 --> 00:27:25,940
tag with durability to match.

424
00:27:25,941 --> 00:27:28,860
The Sali copper is one of the most durable metals that we have.

425
00:27:28,861 --> 00:27:33,240
It will last forever as a casket.

426
00:27:33,241 --> 00:27:38,480
For Thacker caskets, they know their product will be one of the last things families see

427
00:27:38,481 --> 00:27:40,600
before saying goodbye to a loved one.

428
00:27:40,601 --> 00:27:45,500
There are not many places that you build something that you're going to make as beautiful as

429
00:27:45,501 --> 00:27:46,500
our caskets.

430
00:27:46,501 --> 00:27:49,420
And then in a couple of days, you're going to put them in the ground.

431
00:27:49,421 --> 00:27:54,300
But our people take pride in that, knowing that the families have comfort and closure

432
00:27:54,301 --> 00:27:59,520
at the end of the life of a loved one.

433
00:27:59,521 --> 00:28:04,800
There's another future scenario where we could find ourselves in containers underground,

434
00:28:04,801 --> 00:28:08,980
but alive and with all the amenities of home.

435
00:28:08,981 --> 00:28:12,980
But when we get there, we might not be alone.

436
00:28:12,981 --> 00:28:16,700
Next on Modern Marvels, Built to Last.

437
00:28:16,701 --> 00:28:22,740
We'll return to Built to Last on Modern Marvels.

438
00:28:22,741 --> 00:28:28,300
Few things on Earth endure generation after generation.

439
00:28:28,301 --> 00:28:32,000
are built to withstand the centuries,

440
00:28:32,001 --> 00:28:35,620
and the planet's life cycle runs its course.

441
00:28:35,621 --> 00:28:37,720
But thanks to a common myth, some

442
00:28:37,721 --> 00:28:40,780
think that if the end were near and all life forms were

443
00:28:40,781 --> 00:28:43,700
extinguished, only one lucky species

444
00:28:43,701 --> 00:28:47,740
might be genetically built to last, the cockroach.

445
00:28:52,140 --> 00:28:56,240
At his entomology lab at North Carolina State University,

446
00:28:56,241 --> 00:28:59,780
Dr. Coby Schall takes a closer look than most

447
00:28:59,781 --> 00:29:02,580
into why these infamous creatures are so tough.

448
00:29:04,020 --> 00:29:06,120
Some cockroaches are incredibly durable.

449
00:29:06,121 --> 00:29:08,120
Those that, for example, that live in the desert

450
00:29:08,121 --> 00:29:12,220
can withstand dehydration for many weeks at a time.

451
00:29:14,760 --> 00:29:16,720
Another incredible asset,

452
00:29:16,721 --> 00:29:20,100
cockroaches can survive without some body parts.

453
00:29:21,100 --> 00:29:22,960
Cockroaches, like many other insects,

454
00:29:22,961 --> 00:29:26,140
can function, for example, without their brain.

455
00:29:26,141 --> 00:29:27,300
without the head.

456
00:29:27,301 --> 00:29:30,240
When we cut their head off, obviously they miss food,

457
00:29:30,241 --> 00:29:32,420
they miss water.

458
00:29:32,421 --> 00:29:36,020
But otherwise, they can function quite well.

459
00:29:36,021 --> 00:29:38,460
Even decapitated, the body parts

460
00:29:38,461 --> 00:29:41,720
continue to show signs of life.

461
00:29:41,721 --> 00:29:44,700
They can breathe because they breathe through their holes

462
00:29:44,701 --> 00:29:47,660
in their abdomen, and it can do so for about 30 days.

463
00:29:50,840 --> 00:29:54,000
So what about a nuclear scenario?

464
00:29:54,001 --> 00:29:56,400
Would cockroaches be roaming the Earth alone

465
00:29:56,401 --> 00:29:58,500
after such an attack?

466
00:29:58,501 --> 00:30:01,200
What the cockroach has going for it is its behavior.

467
00:30:01,201 --> 00:30:05,880
It likes to hide in places, in cracks, crevices, places

468
00:30:05,881 --> 00:30:07,920
where radiation may not reach.

469
00:30:07,921 --> 00:30:10,400
They don't have bone marrow, which is highly sensitive

470
00:30:10,401 --> 00:30:12,060
to radiation.

471
00:30:12,061 --> 00:30:15,380
But even with the cockroach's biological assets,

472
00:30:15,381 --> 00:30:17,740
Dr. Schall says they're not invincible

473
00:30:17,741 --> 00:30:20,500
in the face of a nuclear foe.

474
00:30:20,501 --> 00:30:23,700
I think it's generally a myth that cockroaches

475
00:30:23,701 --> 00:30:26,640
will out-survive all other life.

476
00:30:26,641 --> 00:30:28,040
They'll survive better than humans,

477
00:30:28,041 --> 00:30:31,700
but a nuclear blast nearby will take out most life.

478
00:30:36,280 --> 00:30:37,940
It's a daunting thought,

479
00:30:37,941 --> 00:30:40,620
and nuclear war never seemed more imminent

480
00:30:40,621 --> 00:30:44,080
than in the mid-20th century.

481
00:30:44,081 --> 00:30:46,580
Soviet advances in the space race

482
00:30:46,581 --> 00:30:49,120
and the Cuban Missile Crisis brought fears

483
00:30:49,121 --> 00:30:52,000
about a nuclear showdown into even sharper focus.

484
00:30:53,700 --> 00:31:03,380
Civil defense campaigns echoed the fears that gripped the population.

485
00:31:04,220 --> 00:31:08,680
And fallout shelters were being touted as a way to ensure our survival,

486
00:31:09,420 --> 00:31:13,080
as seen in this educational film released by the Department of Defense.

487
00:31:14,420 --> 00:31:18,960
Good. You fellas keep the blocks coming. I'm going to set up this wall's eye line.

488
00:31:23,700 --> 00:31:26,140
Hey, you look wonderful, Fred.

489
00:31:26,141 --> 00:31:28,640
Yeah, good job, huh?

490
00:31:28,641 --> 00:31:32,580
This 1955 shelter is now an artifact

491
00:31:32,581 --> 00:31:35,720
at the Smithsonian's National Museum of American History

492
00:31:35,721 --> 00:31:37,680
in Washington.

493
00:31:37,681 --> 00:31:41,980
It was dug up from a front yard in Fort Wayne, Indiana,

494
00:31:41,981 --> 00:31:44,680
and could house a family of four.

495
00:31:44,681 --> 00:31:48,220
The immediate fear that sparked this particular family

496
00:31:48,221 --> 00:31:51,720
to install this shelter was the announcement

497
00:31:51,721 --> 00:31:55,160
that the Russians had exploded a hydrogen bomb.

498
00:31:56,360 --> 00:31:58,400
Shelters like this one were designed

499
00:31:58,401 --> 00:32:01,060
as immediate protection from nuclear fallout.

500
00:32:02,020 --> 00:32:05,420
Early models were effective for a stay of one to two weeks,

501
00:32:05,800 --> 00:32:07,600
even though radiation can be present

502
00:32:07,601 --> 00:32:10,160
up to 30 days after detonation.

503
00:32:10,680 --> 00:32:11,580
There are no utilities.

504
00:32:11,960 --> 00:32:12,940
There's no electricity.

505
00:32:13,700 --> 00:32:15,480
There are batteries and flashlights.

506
00:32:16,420 --> 00:32:17,820
There's no cooking.

507
00:32:18,180 --> 00:32:19,700
There's no running water.

508
00:32:19,701 --> 00:32:22,140
it's pretty confining.

509
00:32:23,640 --> 00:32:27,860
Today, threats of terrorism and unconventional warfare

510
00:32:27,861 --> 00:32:30,680
have caused some to revive the bunker mentality

511
00:32:30,681 --> 00:32:32,180
from America's past.

512
00:32:36,940 --> 00:32:39,260
Radius Engineering in Terrell, Texas

513
00:32:39,261 --> 00:32:42,780
is helping concerned Americans plan for the worst.

514
00:32:43,640 --> 00:32:45,880
This is designed for a nuclear weapon,

515
00:32:46,060 --> 00:32:48,040
a biological weapon, a chemical weapon,

516
00:32:48,041 --> 00:32:50,020
a high altitude of electromagnetic pulse.

517
00:32:50,340 --> 00:32:52,380
There is nothing that this cannot protect you from.

518
00:32:53,300 --> 00:32:55,960
The rare glimpse into shelter life today

519
00:32:55,961 --> 00:32:57,840
may seem like science fiction,

520
00:32:58,400 --> 00:33:00,280
but Radius claims to have installations

521
00:33:00,281 --> 00:33:01,960
in every major U.S. city.

522
00:33:03,060 --> 00:33:05,680
Their biggest customers, corporations,

523
00:33:06,220 --> 00:33:07,740
and a number of federal agencies.

524
00:33:08,320 --> 00:33:10,760
These are loaded with their medicines, if they have them,

525
00:33:10,880 --> 00:33:12,800
their legal files, their photos,

526
00:33:13,280 --> 00:33:14,480
their clothing, their food.

527
00:33:14,700 --> 00:33:15,720
Everything is in here.

528
00:33:15,721 --> 00:33:17,860
They just have to get their body to the shelter.

529
00:33:18,040 --> 00:33:23,040
The shelters are installed eight and a half feet underground

530
00:33:23,041 --> 00:33:25,040
and connect to the surface with a hatch.

531
00:33:25,041 --> 00:33:31,040
A stairway often leads down to the subterranean living space.

532
00:33:31,041 --> 00:33:37,040
Once inside, Radius says inhabitants would be protected from blasts

533
00:33:37,041 --> 00:33:41,040
from a one-megaton weapon up to one mile away.

534
00:33:41,041 --> 00:33:50,820
Unlike early shelters, today's models are far more than just a temporary hideout.

535
00:33:51,080 --> 00:33:53,700
There's bunks in here for 25 people.

536
00:33:54,400 --> 00:33:55,780
There's storage under the floor.

537
00:33:56,560 --> 00:34:01,020
Under this floor, we can store not quite 9,000 cans of food.

538
00:34:02,260 --> 00:34:09,300
Depending on size and food storage, these units are built to sustain human life for an entire year.

539
00:34:11,040 --> 00:34:17,440
You have to have not only an electrical system on AC, you have an electrical system on DC.

540
00:34:17,441 --> 00:34:21,760
You have an air filtration system, which is a life support system, and you have to be

541
00:34:21,761 --> 00:34:24,960
able to do this all off the grid.

542
00:34:24,961 --> 00:34:29,020
The structures rely on curved fiberglass casts.

543
00:34:29,021 --> 00:34:35,920
According to Radius, the fiberglass will last for 300 years and has the resiliency to adjust

544
00:34:35,921 --> 00:34:39,280
to ground movements from a weapons blast.

545
00:34:39,281 --> 00:34:41,320
All of our shelters are always connected by us

546
00:34:41,321 --> 00:34:43,280
through a triple-axis seismic joint.

547
00:34:43,281 --> 00:34:46,660
This allows this flange to flex back and forth.

548
00:34:46,661 --> 00:34:48,280
The entranceway that's connected on here

549
00:34:48,281 --> 00:34:51,620
can wave back and forth, up and down.

550
00:34:51,621 --> 00:34:55,600
The seismic joints easily link the pods together,

551
00:34:55,601 --> 00:34:58,400
and Radius designs even larger shelters

552
00:34:58,401 --> 00:35:01,400
that can interconnect to create entire underground

553
00:35:01,401 --> 00:35:02,400
neighborhoods.

554
00:35:02,401 --> 00:35:12,640
The roomier models are so big, they must be delivered piece by piece.

555
00:35:17,380 --> 00:35:18,840
A dome is 12 segments.

556
00:35:19,420 --> 00:35:20,120
This is one.

557
00:35:21,660 --> 00:35:25,000
This one's going to Washington, D.C., which is where a lot of our business is.

558
00:35:26,040 --> 00:35:30,720
During the Cold War, many shelter owners wanted to keep their construction a secret,

559
00:35:30,721 --> 00:35:33,720
Fearing neighbors might come knocking in a crisis.

560
00:35:33,721 --> 00:35:35,720
Looks like you've been busy.

561
00:35:35,721 --> 00:35:39,720
Still today, many clients demand anonymity.

562
00:35:39,721 --> 00:35:42,720
It's kind of a stealth operation.

563
00:35:42,721 --> 00:35:44,720
Secrecy is important to all of our clients,

564
00:35:44,721 --> 00:35:46,720
especially the ones in D.C.

565
00:35:50,720 --> 00:35:53,720
For customers receiving the larger shipments,

566
00:35:53,721 --> 00:35:56,720
it's the shelter equivalent of the condo.

567
00:35:56,721 --> 00:35:59,720
This has got a full eight-foot ceiling.

568
00:35:59,721 --> 00:36:02,780
We have a fairly conventional kitchen.

569
00:36:03,120 --> 00:36:05,280
We have a utility sink.

570
00:36:05,840 --> 00:36:08,820
We've got electric heating elements here for cooking.

571
00:36:09,200 --> 00:36:10,780
The kids can play out here,

572
00:36:11,340 --> 00:36:14,840
and the rest of the family can go upstairs.

573
00:36:15,660 --> 00:36:18,780
The clamshell design allows space for more supplies

574
00:36:18,781 --> 00:36:22,380
and stays of up to five years.

575
00:36:23,640 --> 00:36:26,880
The secret installation in these exclusive photos

576
00:36:26,881 --> 00:36:29,380
is in the Washington, D.C. region.

577
00:36:29,720 --> 00:36:32,920
It carried a price tag of $2 million

578
00:36:32,921 --> 00:36:36,160
and will include a gym and a radio station.

579
00:36:36,161 --> 00:36:40,260
A lot of the customers wanted a different lifestyle.

580
00:36:40,261 --> 00:36:44,660
They wanted more volume and they wanted longer term.

581
00:36:44,661 --> 00:36:47,780
Underground shelters might last for centuries,

582
00:36:47,781 --> 00:36:51,080
but what if we wanted to protect not just ourselves,

583
00:36:51,081 --> 00:36:54,480
but one of the nation's most prized possessions?

584
00:36:54,481 --> 00:36:56,920
Thanks to some high-tech engineering,

585
00:36:56,921 --> 00:37:00,380
Life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness

586
00:37:00,381 --> 00:37:01,880
have a promising future.

587
00:37:05,880 --> 00:37:10,380
By the mid-1960s, some 200,000 home fallout shelters

588
00:37:10,381 --> 00:37:12,880
had reportedly been built in the United States.

589
00:37:14,960 --> 00:37:18,260
We now return to Built to Last on Modern Marvels.

590
00:37:21,880 --> 00:37:25,120
Around the world, structures built for the ages

591
00:37:25,121 --> 00:37:27,680
pay lasting tribute to ancient competitions,

592
00:37:29,020 --> 00:37:30,120
ceremonial burials,

593
00:37:30,660 --> 00:37:34,020
and glorious efforts to keep out invaders.

594
00:37:35,260 --> 00:37:38,040
But there's one building in Washington, D.C.

595
00:37:38,340 --> 00:37:40,700
that honors not just human achievement,

596
00:37:41,280 --> 00:37:44,080
but the very ideals that established a nation.

597
00:37:45,840 --> 00:37:47,880
Inside the National Archives,

598
00:37:48,280 --> 00:37:49,460
lights are kept to a minimum

599
00:37:49,461 --> 00:37:51,580
to protect the U.S. Constitution,

600
00:37:52,480 --> 00:37:53,500
the Bill of Rights,

601
00:37:53,501 --> 00:37:57,640
and the most cherished document, the Declaration of Independence.

602
00:37:58,280 --> 00:38:02,980
But this beloved document is widely considered the most fragile.

603
00:38:03,240 --> 00:38:07,320
The Declaration is, of all of the Charters of Freedom documents, the most faded.

604
00:38:07,460 --> 00:38:09,360
It's kind of the saddest to look at.

605
00:38:10,060 --> 00:38:12,800
The case which houses the Declaration today

606
00:38:12,801 --> 00:38:16,380
was built to give lasting protection to this national treasure.

607
00:38:16,960 --> 00:38:20,760
But it's a recent addition to its more than 200-year lifespan.

608
00:38:20,761 --> 00:38:25,760
For the document's initial unveiling at the archives in 1952,

609
00:38:25,761 --> 00:38:29,760
a special hermetically sealed case was constructed.

610
00:38:29,761 --> 00:38:33,760
Helium, an inert gas that would not react with the paper,

611
00:38:33,761 --> 00:38:35,760
filled the case.

612
00:38:35,761 --> 00:38:38,760
I think it's only fair to say that in the 1950s,

613
00:38:38,761 --> 00:38:41,760
they did the very best they could with existing knowledge

614
00:38:41,761 --> 00:38:43,760
and current technology.

615
00:38:45,760 --> 00:38:49,760
Each night, the case was lowered into an underground vault,

616
00:38:49,761 --> 00:38:52,460
vault built to survive an atomic bomb.

617
00:38:55,240 --> 00:38:58,800
But the design of the case would ultimately raise concerns

618
00:38:58,801 --> 00:39:02,440
decades later, particularly over the glass.

619
00:39:02,441 --> 00:39:05,280
Its positioning directly on the parchment

620
00:39:05,281 --> 00:39:09,220
was one of the many aspects that troubled conservators.

621
00:39:09,221 --> 00:39:12,180
We were also becoming aware that there were problems

622
00:39:12,181 --> 00:39:16,260
with degradation of the inner layers of glass.

623
00:39:16,261 --> 00:39:19,320
So that made us think that perhaps the conditions

624
00:39:19,321 --> 00:39:22,260
inside of the encasement were changing

625
00:39:22,261 --> 00:39:26,720
and perhaps could be harmful over time to the documents.

626
00:39:26,721 --> 00:39:29,860
In 1995, the National Archives decided

627
00:39:29,861 --> 00:39:32,000
a longer lasting home for the Declaration

628
00:39:32,001 --> 00:39:33,660
was urgently needed.

629
00:39:33,661 --> 00:39:37,180
In 2001, they opened the case for the first time

630
00:39:37,181 --> 00:39:38,340
in half a century.

631
00:39:38,341 --> 00:39:41,240
It was a little bit scary because we didn't know

632
00:39:41,241 --> 00:39:43,040
exactly what was going to happen.

633
00:39:43,041 --> 00:39:45,740
We were always concerned about whether there would be

634
00:39:45,741 --> 00:39:50,780
any adhering of the parchment to that cover glass.

635
00:39:50,781 --> 00:39:53,280
The document performed well, but the glass

636
00:39:53,281 --> 00:39:56,620
showed degradation, and the parchment's faded condition

637
00:39:56,621 --> 00:39:58,240
was striking.

638
00:39:58,241 --> 00:40:00,680
There's hardly any ink left.

639
00:40:00,681 --> 00:40:03,140
It's as though it was washed away.

640
00:40:03,141 --> 00:40:05,220
Its weathered condition was a testament

641
00:40:05,221 --> 00:40:07,220
to the harrowing journey that brought it here.

642
00:40:09,640 --> 00:40:11,780
Despite depictions to the contrary,

643
00:40:11,781 --> 00:40:14,740
historians believe the founding fathers signed the documents

644
00:40:14,741 --> 00:40:19,980
separately, beginning its repeated handling over time.

645
00:40:19,981 --> 00:40:22,680
The declaration then traveled, rolled up,

646
00:40:22,681 --> 00:40:27,980
with the Continental Congress during the Revolutionary War.

647
00:40:27,981 --> 00:40:30,300
The assault continued in the 1800s,

648
00:40:30,301 --> 00:40:32,980
when it hung for 35 years on display

649
00:40:32,981 --> 00:40:36,640
in a sun-drenched window in Washington.

650
00:40:36,641 --> 00:40:38,760
To help rescue the prized parchment,

651
00:40:38,761 --> 00:40:42,100
the archives turned to NIST, the National Institute

652
00:40:42,101 --> 00:40:45,140
standards and technology.

653
00:40:45,141 --> 00:40:49,888
In their labs outside Washington, NIST engineers worked to support U.S.

654
00:40:49,889 --> 00:40:51,560
innovation and industrial

655
00:40:51,561 --> 00:40:54,560
competitiveness.

656
00:40:54,561 --> 00:41:00,000
NIST had worked on the original case, and it was here that the declaration's new home

657
00:41:00,001 --> 00:41:01,800
came to life.

658
00:41:01,801 --> 00:41:07,080
The case started out as a 600-pound aluminum block at this milling machine.

659
00:41:07,081 --> 00:41:12,540
This was a prototype that was made prior to the ones that you see now down at the National

660
00:41:12,541 --> 00:41:13,540
Archives.

661
00:41:13,541 --> 00:41:18,360
We took this solid block of material, bolted it down to the milling machine, and then programmed

662
00:41:18,361 --> 00:41:23,240
the cutter where it came down and it cut out all the extra material out.

663
00:41:23,241 --> 00:41:28,520
Weight had to be reduced while still maintaining the long-term strength of the metal.

664
00:41:28,521 --> 00:41:35,360
You can see that we have machined little pockets, and these pockets take away weight but don't

665
00:41:35,361 --> 00:41:38,800
reduce the strength or the stiffness of the base very much.

666
00:41:39,360 --> 00:41:44,140
Valves on the underside would pump in a more effective gas with greater stamina.

667
00:41:44,580 --> 00:41:46,013
We changed the gas.

668
00:41:46,014 --> 00:41:51,140
The inert gas is now argon, which is a smaller molecule than helium,

669
00:41:51,300 --> 00:41:52,860
so less likely to leak out.

670
00:41:53,300 --> 00:41:58,560
The case was polished to a microscopic level, and prototypes were tested for their ability

671
00:41:58,561 --> 00:42:01,980
to withstand air pressure changes in case of a tornado.

672
00:42:01,981 --> 00:42:06,080
The glass that was decided on was a laminated safety glass,

673
00:42:06,180 --> 00:42:10,420
like in a car window, except a little bit thicker.

674
00:42:10,520 --> 00:42:13,020
The glass lasted longer than expected

675
00:42:13,120 --> 00:42:15,820
and passed the stress test.

676
00:42:15,920 --> 00:42:19,740
And the critical final piece, the seal.

677
00:42:19,840 --> 00:42:22,640
A thin groove was carefully cut into the encasement,

678
00:42:22,740 --> 00:42:26,380
where the glass and aluminum would marry.

679
00:42:26,480 --> 00:42:30,380
The seal is a very important part of this encasement

680
00:42:30,381 --> 00:42:35,580
because it's what provides the seal that we say is going to last 100 years,

681
00:42:35,581 --> 00:42:40,220
is this metal seal that keeps the air out for a long, long time.

682
00:42:40,221 --> 00:42:45,560
The Declaration of Independence was introduced to its new home in 2003.

683
00:42:45,561 --> 00:42:49,100
And for the archives, the improvements were striking.

684
00:42:49,101 --> 00:42:54,780
The glass for the encasement is now at least an inch or so off the surface of the document.

685
00:42:54,781 --> 00:42:59,100
We have an airtight encasement, so oxygen cannot enter in,

686
00:42:59,101 --> 00:43:02,600
so there cannot be any chemical degradation of the document.

687
00:43:03,880 --> 00:43:09,320
For everyone involved, the final product meant a piece of history would safely endure.

688
00:43:10,240 --> 00:43:17,180
The Declaration of Independence encasement, we think, is a permanent home for the document,

689
00:43:17,320 --> 00:43:19,260
and we think it will last a long, long time.

690
00:43:19,920 --> 00:43:24,260
The newly fortified encasement sits proudly in the archives today.

691
00:43:24,261 --> 00:43:31,020
preserved, protected, and rescued from fading any further at the hands of time.

692
00:43:31,980 --> 00:43:34,780
In its encasement, it's incredibly beautiful.

693
00:43:35,880 --> 00:43:40,160
The display serves as a reminder that while time may take its toll,

694
00:43:40,580 --> 00:43:43,960
lasting creations and ideals can still prevail.

695
00:43:45,140 --> 00:43:50,340
Throughout history, civilization has been trying to build things to last through the ages.

696
00:43:50,341 --> 00:43:55,220
And with technology's help, we're getting better at it every day.

